WEBVTT

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.580
Hi, my name's Sam. If you don't know me, I'm just a mere, humble resident pastor here.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.260
It's my privilege to get to share the word with you today.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.160
And as we get started, I don't know how many of you know, but earlier this semester,

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:25.760
Stanford University found themselves in a little bit of a scandal-slash-situation.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.900
What had happened was they, on their marketing materials,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.080
They took this picture that originally had this Asian woman, this white guy, and a Latino student,

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:42.800
and they AI edited it to transform this Latino student into an African-American female.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.000
You see it up here.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.280
And they got caught.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.580
They got caught trying to fabricate this kind of diversity for their promotional materials.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.700
People called them out.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.440
They also look skinnier and a little bit more attractive in the AI editing.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.140
That's a whole different sermon about beauty standards and our value in Christ.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.900
But anyway, they got caught for this.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.720
And it's not the first time this has happened.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.520
Earlier in 2019, a college in Pennsylvania similarly took one photo.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.520
They took it, and then they decided it didn't have enough diversity.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.560
So they got other students to fill in, and then they switched them out.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.220
And again, they got caught because people were like, hey, I was there at that photo shoot.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.680
Those people weren't there, right?

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.560
And this has happened multiple times.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.860
In 2018, a school, a college in France,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.400
on the American version of their website,

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.700
they were trying to change their pictures to look more diverse.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.620
So they made certain people look darker.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:41.600
They substituted other people in that weren't there.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.820
And it happens, unfortunately, enough, right?

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.980
And then the granddaddy of them all in 2000, way back,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.840
this is probably when Photoshop was still really hard to do and learn,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.860
on the cover of one of their admissions marketing magazines,

00:01:54.860 --> 00:02:00.940
University of Wisconsin-Madison, they photoshopped this black guy into the cover.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.500
And the way they got caught, apparently, was they were like, oh, yo, I saw you.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.080
You were, like, on the cover, like, at the football game.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.120
And this guy was like, I've never been to a football game, right?

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.980
And so the question is, like, why do these things happen?

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:18.080
Why do these things happen with some regularity, right?

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.300
I think the reality is we know, some of us, we know this reality where the university, right,

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.020
They want to be diverse.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.480
They want to seem diverse.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.780
They want to appear diverse.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:35.600
But that reality is not there on their campus, and so they have to edit, they have to fabricate

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.780
that reality.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.060
And I think all the more, you know, we get frustrated because it's not real because it's

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:40.320
edited.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.960
It's not even like you got real students to be overrepresented in the picture.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.980
It's like you just fake added people there.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:53.120
I think many of us know this tension of like wanting diversity, but being in a context where

00:02:53.120 --> 00:03:00.360
they're not making it a reality, wanting to be united, but not actually seeing that diversity

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:06.320
there. Now, thankfully, actually for younger people, I think the younger generations,

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.020
it seems that actually younger people are more open to ethnic and racial diversity. I think

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:17.920
across the board, Gen Z, Gen Alpha are more comfortable with the idea of ethnic diversity,

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:23.100
and their friend groups might be more diverse than before, but I think they're still

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:30.400
challenges with diversity, especially with things like political diversity. Not too long ago on the

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:37.800
U of M campus, there was an incident where a student attacked this conservative group that

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:44.420
was on the Diag unpermitted. And these hostilities, maybe not ethnic, but these political hostilities

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:51.540
have been growing more and more. We also have friends. This was kind of worrisome and endearing.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:58.800
We have friends, and their kids were scared to talk to us because they were, like, they were asking their parents, like, are they Republican?

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.560
Like, because they're from Michigan, and in their mind, Michigan's, like, Republican.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.540
Like, are they Republican? Like, I'm scared to talk to them, right?

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:12.420
And so even if younger generations might be more comfortable with ethnic diversity, there's a concern about do we have real political diversity?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.060
Are we okay with people who believe things that are different from us?

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.360
See, it's getting real, right?

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.940
And so there's this challenge where in our lives we see this diversity as a good thing.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.060
We desire it.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.340
We want to be a part of it.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:29.000
But we also like keeping to people who are like ourselves, right?

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:34.560
It's hard to make the actual effort to get to know people and to love people who are actually very different from you.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:40.940
And so the question is, when we turn to the Bible, what does the Bible have to say about unity in the midst of diversity?

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.140
How should it be for the church?

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:51.280
in psalm 133 it says how good is it when brothers dwell in unity and so today's message is titled

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:57.700
learning to dwell in unity and to see how does it look how is it possible that the church can be a

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:03.480
place where there's unity but also diversity and so the we'll see as we look into this passage for

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.940
today that god has created this reconciled and diverse community and that we're going to live

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.480
in light of that. That's why the one thing is that we are called to live out the unity that God

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:20.380
established for his diverse people. That we're called to live out the unity that God established

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.340
for his diverse people. This is what it says in Ephesians chapter 2, verse 11 to 22.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:32.100
Therefore, remember that at one time you Gentiles in the flesh, called the uncircumcision by what

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.720
is called the circumcision, which is made in the flesh by hands, remember that you were at that

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.620
time separated from Christ, alienated from the commonwealth of Israel, and strangers to the

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:46.600
covenants of promise, having no hope and without God in the world. But now in Christ's shoes as

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:52.660
you who were once far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ. For he himself is our

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:58.940
peace who has made us both one and broken down in his flesh the dividing wall of hostility by

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:04.140
abolishing the law of commandments expressed in ordinances that he might create in himself one new

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:10.540
man in place of the two, so making peace, and might reconcile us both to God in one body through the

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:16.800
cross, thereby killing the hostility. And he came and preached to you who were far off, and preached

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:22.680
to those who were near. For through him we both have access in one spirit to the Father. So then you

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:28.340
are no longer strangers and aliens, but you are fellow citizens with the saints, and members of

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:33.520
the household of God, built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets Christ Jesus himself

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.560
being the cornerstone in whom the whole structure being joined together grows into a holy temple

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:44.400
in the Lord. In him you are also being built together into a dwelling place for God

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.680
by the Spirit. Please join me as we continue on in this message together. Father, we come to you

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:57.260
today. We ask that you would open our eyes, open our ears, soften our hearts, that we might hear

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.540
your word preached to us,

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.600
that your Holy Spirit would apply it into our lives,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.380
and that you would help us to see areas of our lives

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:08.040
where we need to repent,

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.680
areas where we need to be encouraged,

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.860
and areas where we need to be equipped to live for you.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.440
We thank you for meeting us here.

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.620
We thank you for your constant grace

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.000
and mercy and pursuit of us.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:19.560
We thank you.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.720
We pray these things in Jesus' name.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:21.120
Amen.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:22.800
Amen.

